    "article_content_rVJxjW": {
      "type": "article__content",
      "blocks": {
        "text_iXXH6r": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-md",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-md",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-sm",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-lg",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-xl",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "95ch",
            "richtext": "<p>When colds and flu are going around, an air purifier for viruses and bacteria is one way to improve indoor air quality and help keep you and your family healthy.⁰ Blueair purifiers remove viruses and bacteria from the air and kill germs,¹ but no air purifier can offer complete protection against Coronavirus.² Blueair does not claim that its air purifiers capture, remove, or kill COVID-19 (SARS-CoV-2). Keep reading to learn more about air purifiers for viruses.<br\/><br\/><strong>See Blueair's Response to COVID-19 for more<\/strong><br\/><\/p>",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_U99YtL": {
          "type": "image_caption",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "80ch",
            "image": "shopify:\/\/shop_images\/Particle_chart_distance_v2-02.png",
            "image_desktop": "shopify:\/\/shop_images\/Particle_chart_distance_v2-02.png",
            "caption": "",
            "caption_style": "p6",
            "product": ""
          }
        },
        "text_RKxLgn": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-md",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-md",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "95ch",
            "richtext": "<h6>How airborne viruses and bacteria spread<\/h6><p>Viruses and bacteria can both be spread through airborne transmission. Bacterial infections with airborne transmission include Strep throat, tuberculosis, and other illnesses. While viral infections include chickenpox, the common cold, and the flu. <\/p><p>Humans are commonly responsible for spreading viruses and diseases to others in the home, workplace, and any other area with crowded conditions and poor air circulation.³ When someone sneezes or coughs, tiny water or mucous droplets (commonly called respiratory droplets) filled with viruses or bacteria scatter in the air. Depending on the droplets' size (5-10 µm), germs can stay suspended in the air for a few minutes or even hours. For this reason, infections are more likely to spread in stagnant air. Improved ventilation and clean air circulation can dilute airborne contaminants to make a meaningful impact on the indoor spread of diseases.⁴<\/p>",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
